                <h2>Question 1: How should budget be split across Google, Meta, and TikTok?</h2>
<p>There is no universal split. If branded searches already have stable organic demand, allocate about 30% to Google Shopping or brand-related non-brand campaigns. Meta can handle retargeting and interest-based prospecting, usually receiving 30%–40%, while TikTok is worth 15%–25% for visual products, younger audiences, and short-form video. Keep roughly 10% for new-product tests, coupon validation, and temporary scaling.</p>
<p>These percentages are only a starting point. A store with high order values and long repurchase cycles should not copy the allocation of a low-value, high-frequency store. The real question is whether the contribution profit from new customers covers advertising, fulfillment, and other variable costs—not whether the platform-reported ROAS looks acceptable.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>An independent tool website initially placed 60% of its budget on Meta. Its clicks were inexpensive, but post-purchase refunds were high. Adding real use cases, specifications, and customer reviews improved the effective conversion rate, allowing the team to scale Meta gradually.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Question 2: Which platform should receive priority when the budget is limited?</h2>
<p>Prioritize the channel closest to generating profitable orders, not necessarily the channel with the largest reach. Review the past 14 days of data, including product-page-to-cart conversion, cart-to-payment conversion, refund rates, and coverage of target markets. If a channel already produces high-quality traffic but is budget-constrained, increase its allocation. If it generates substantial traffic with a below-average payment conversion rate, fix pricing, shipping terms, payment options, or trust signals before spending more.</p>
<p>For a new store starting with limited funds, a practical sequence is Google high-intent search or Shopping, followed by Meta retargeting, with TikTok tested progressively. This approach helps validate search demand and product-market fit faster without ruling out short-video channels.</p>
<h2>Question 3: How should the first-month testing budget be set to avoid burning through it too quickly?</h2>
<p>Divide the first-month budget into a testing pool and a scaling pool—for example, 70% and 30%. Apply weekly spending limits to the test pool, and reserve the scaling pool for validated combinations of creative, product, and market.</p>
<p>When calculating an affordable CAC, do not use gross margin minus ad spend alone. Include discounts, payment fees, fulfillment, customer service, and expected refunds. One ecommerce team initially set its CAC target at 20% of gross margin and exceeded the acceptable acquisition limit. Switching to roughly 60%–70% of first-order contribution profit reduced early-stage losses.</p>
<p>Test at least two variable types: creative angle and audience or market. Do not change budget, bid, landing page, and promotion simultaneously. Otherwise, even if performance improves, the team will not know which change caused the result.</p>
<h2>Question 4: When should ads that perform well receive more budget?</h2>
<p>Do not double the budget solely because ROAS rose for one day. A stronger scaling condition is 7–14 consecutive days at the target CAC, stable payment conversion, no simultaneous increase in refunds or cancellations, and sufficient inventory and fulfillment capacity. Start with a 20% increase, then monitor marginal CAC over the next 24–48 hours instead of relying only on average metrics.</p>
<p>Set downgrade rules as well. Pause a creative combination if it receives 2–3 times the required conversion sample without producing valid orders. If marginal CAC keeps rising and contribution profit turns negative, restore the previous budget. Effective budget management is a continuous cycle of stopping losses, scaling winners, and reallocating spend.</p>
<h2>Question 5: How should finance and account operations support the advertising budget?</h2>
<p>Maintain a separate budget sheet for every platform, legal entity, and market. At minimum, track top-ups, ad spend, platform balances, actual order revenue, refunds, and profit attributed to advertising. Avoid loading the full balance at once. Recharge according to the next two weeks of expected consumption and retain a 10%–20% cash buffer so a failed payment does not interrupt campaigns.</p>
<p>For overseas ad accounts, enforce spending permissions, billing alerts, and balance thresholds. When managing multiple accounts, do not use budget transfers as the only scaling method. First confirm account health, payment method status, and creative approval to prevent budget waste on restricted or underperforming accounts. Reconcile platform invoices against payment gateway orders and actual refunds rather than relying solely on platform-reported conversions.</p>
<h2>A Practical Weekly Budget Adjustment Process</h2>
<p>Complete four tasks every week: calculate new-customer contribution profit by channel, identify combinations that meet the CAC target but lack sufficient budget, check refund, chargeback, and fulfillment anomalies, and then decide whether to scale, downgrade, or pause. A reliable budget sheet should include four separate fields: spend, confirmed revenue, pending revenue, and actual contribution profit. This prevents inflated platform ROAS from hiding long-term losses.</p>
<p>The objective is not to spend the entire budget. It is to build sustainable order growth within an affordable CAC. Once the independent site reaches a stable stage, allocate budget dynamically according to profit contribution rather than keeping platform percentages fixed.</p>
